WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.330
An unknown alliance, a secret eff you, and
a damning hint towards the future?

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.799
There’s secrets stitched into these sci-fi
threads!

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:10.844
So which one plays homage to detectives and
suffragettes?

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:15.440
Wil Wheaton's Wesley Crusher made his Star
Trek debut in an episode of The Next Generation

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.765
in 1987.

00:00:16.765 --> 00:00:21.920
The son of Dr. Beverly Crusher, he's long
been one of the fandom's most divisive characters.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.039
Some found him annoying.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:25.390
Others found his super-genius skills preposterous.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:30.310
And the rest just didn't like the idea of
a teenager on the bridge of the Enterprise.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.300
Nevertheless, Crusher received the provisional
rank of Acting Ensign in the episode "Where

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.390
No One Has Gone Before" along with his own
special uniform.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:41.260
Acting Ensign Wesley Crusher's costume – an
awkward gray-toned turtleneck with a rainbow

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.850
stripe that wraps around the shoulders – set
him apart from the rest of the crew.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.719
It also held a secret message.

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:50.170
The colorful stripe across his chest is made
up of all three divisional colors worn by

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.530
the rest of the crew.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:55.460
Red represents the command division, yellow
is for security and operations, and blue is

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.920
for science and medical.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.780
Crusher wears all three to denote that, as
a provisional officer, he hasn't yet chosen

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.620
a division.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.546
Jodi Whittaker Thirteenth Doctor on Doctor
Who faced sexist backlash following her casting,

00:01:06.546 --> 00:01:09.226
but few found fault with her colorful, offbeat
outfit.

00:01:09.226 --> 00:01:13.870
Whittaker first appeared in a blue shirt with
a rainbow stripe, a long gray coat, and suspenders

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:17.370
holding up a pair of blue trousers, which
gave the Thirteenth Doctor the right mix of

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.890
style and quirkiness.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.990
But while fans noticed subtle homages to the
past — the Fourth Doctor's colorful stripes,

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.630
the Eleventh Doctor's bow ties, and the high-waisted
pants of the Second Doctor — there's more.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.159
For example, the blue and gray represent the
sky and space.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:33.816
In a behind-the-scenes featurette, Whittaker
pointed to her earrings, saying,

00:01:33.816 --> 00:01:38.747
"And then there's a whole journey of stars
and hands in the earring.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.469
The chain is the journey, and the rose gold
hand is humanity."

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:47.560
Costume designer Ray Holman added a violet
lining to her coat sleeves because it was

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.950
one of the colors of the suffragettes, the
activists who fought for women's votes in

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:51.950
the United Kingdom.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.845
For Whittaker, it was important that her costume
not be overly feminine or masculine.

00:01:55.845 --> 00:01:56.845
She explained,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.469
"The main journey of it came from a photograph
that I found of a woman in trousers, braces,

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:05.549
and a t-shirt walking with purpose."

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:09.606
Whittaker loved its intrigue and timelessness,
and the Thirteenth Doctor's style was born.

00:02:09.606 --> 00:02:14.319
A genre-breaking superhero movie that shattered
long-held Hollywood perceptions, Black Panther

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:17.780
saw the late Chadwick Boseman lead the people
of Wakanda to glory.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.920
Wakanda is home to a rare element called Vibranium,
which allows the country to develop futuristic

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.600
technology while still honoring the people's
culture and traditions.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.080
Costume designer Ruth E. Carter created many
of the futuristic outfits seen in the film

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.310
to pay homage to Africa's past.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:35.700
Inspired by the style called Afrofuturism,
Carter created some 700 unique costumes for

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.466
the movie, including ceremonial dress and
the warrior armor worn by the likes of Okoye.

00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:47.920
"We crafted a costume that really did represent
all the areas of Africa."

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.110
For example, Carter incorporated beadwork
from the Turkana tribe and leather skirts

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.300
wrapped the way the women of the Himba tribe
dress.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.620
In an interview with NPR, she explained,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.750
"I really wanted this to have a feeling that,
if you were an aspiring Dora Milaje – if

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.690
you were training and you were granted the
position to be a member, that you would be

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.170
presented with this honor and this beautiful
uniform."

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:10.220
"It's a great honor for someone to be a Dora
Milaje.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:15.080
They also wear neck rings and arm rings that
are from South Africa."

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:20.400
For the 2022 sequel, Wakanda Forever, Carter
designed another 2,000 outfits, including

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.840
the Midnight Angel armor worn by Okoye.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.780
Using the original comic book art as a starting
point, real African designs were added.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.030
She told Entertainment Weekly,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.560
"We landed on a mask style that's very much
like the masks of Benin or South Africa, where

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.580
you see this slanted eye with a round shape."

00:03:35.077 --> 00:03:38.730
The Back to the Future trilogy didn't require
much elaborate costuming.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.230
The first and third films are set in the past,
but 1989's Back to the Future Part II plays

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.350
out in the far-off future of 2015.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:49.921
Oddly enough, though, it's not the slick,
futuristic fashion worn by Marty McFly, or

00:03:49.921 --> 00:03:53.939
the cyber-punk costumes of Biff Tannen and
his cronies that hide some hidden meaning.

00:03:53.939 --> 00:03:57.450
Instead, it's the more ordinary attire of
scientist Doc Brown.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.840
Throughout the film, Brown wears a bright,
loosely fitted button-down shirt.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.980
But look closely and you'll see the shirt
doesn't feature the Hawaiian flowers you might

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.160
expect, but a repeating pattern depicting
a steam locomotive.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.970
It's no coincidence, of course, that in the
third and final film in the series, Marty

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.240
and Doc Brown hijack just such a train to
carry them back to the future.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.071
Later, Brown even uses a locomotive as a time
machine, and here it was, a year earlier,

00:04:19.071 --> 00:04:20.687
being foreshadowed in his shirt.

00:04:20.687 --> 00:04:21.787
But that's not all.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.009
Pay close attention to the train hijacking
scene in Back to the Future Part III and you'll

00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:29.740
see that Doc uses the same shirt — torn
up — as his face covering while playing

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.011
a gun-toting bandit.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.710
A mind-bending sci-fi journey through the
Wild West — at least at first — Westworld

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.490
is peppered with clues for the audience.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.580
Viewers pore over every scene hunting for
details that may hold some significance.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.220
And the cast's elaborate costumes are always
a good place to start.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:51.449
"The architecture guided the costumes, so
there's a lack of detail to most of our costumes

00:04:51.449 --> 00:04:52.988
that's intentional."

00:04:52.988 --> 00:04:56.509
While costume designer Shay Cunliffe insists
that many of the clothing decisions weren't

00:04:56.509 --> 00:05:01.296
as deliberate as some fans believe, they were
absolutely influenced by the story's deeper themes.

00:05:01.296 --> 00:05:03.923
But there was at least one choice that hid
a deeper meaning.

00:05:03.923 --> 00:05:08.230
In Season 3, when Dolores returns, the many
blue outfits are a nod to the iconic blue

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:11.180
dress she wore inside the park in the first
two seasons.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.400
Also, when Clementine returns — now in the
outside world — her post-modern dress matches

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.889
her flowing gown from inside the Westworld
park.

00:05:17.889 --> 00:05:21.342
In an interview with Insider, Cunliffe referred
to such callbacks as part of a "theme-song

00:05:21.342 --> 00:05:24.000
of color" that ran throughout the series.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.240
Based on the seminal novel by Margaret Atwood,
The Handmaid's Tale takes place in a grim

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.199
near future in which the United States has
become an authoritarian society.

00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:35.630
Women are a subjugated lower class in bland
robes and bonnet-like head coverings that

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.916
are color coded based on their role in society.

00:05:37.916 --> 00:05:39.352
Handmaids wear blood red.

00:05:39.352 --> 00:05:40.789
Wives wear royal blue.

00:05:40.789 --> 00:05:42.870
Domestic workers wear green, and so on.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.910
The outfits are designed to show the women's
complete lack of power.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.150
Costume designer Ane Crabtree told Vanity
Fair,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.860
"It was kind of twisted to think about how
I would hinder women — their body shape,

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.139
and also their movement and their freedom
with the clothing.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:57.220
I had to do it to make it realistic, but also
to help the actors."

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.319
"You feel blinkered.

00:05:58.319 --> 00:05:59.398
Completely blinkered"

00:05:59.398 --> 00:06:03.820
She went on to explain that she took a psychological
approach to the color-coded system, saying,

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.530
"There's less and less power as you go down
the color spectrum, except with the color

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.930
red — that is by design from the book."

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.889
Her job was to create outfits designed by
oppressors, though she admitted she hid a

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:16.870
couple of things in the designs that were
meant to be a "f— you" to the men keeping

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:17.888
these women down.

00:06:17.888 --> 00:06:18.888
She confessed,

00:06:18.888 --> 00:06:22.478
"I did an inverted female vagina in the design
of the Aunt's collars.

00:06:22.478 --> 00:06:24.424
If you look subtly, you'll see that."

00:06:24.424 --> 00:06:28.560
It's no coincidence that the costumes worn
by the authoritarian Terran Federation in

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.020
Starship Troopers recall those worn by high-ranking
Nazi officers during World War II.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:36.759
Over the years, director Paul Verhoeven has
spoken about how the film, which is based

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:41.150
on the Robert A. Heinlein novel of the same
name, is a satire of military dictatorships

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:42.500
like the Third Reich.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.720
For example, Carl Jenkins, played by a young
Neil Patrick Harris, wears a uniform that

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.310
starkly invokes the Gestapo, Adolf Hitler's
secret police.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:54.290
Rather than glorifying authoritarianism as
many critics wrongly assumed upon the film's

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:58.479
release, the uniforms were meant to be a subtle
takedown of the frightening ways authoritarian

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:00.300
regimes justify their brutality.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.060
Verhoeven told Entertainment Weekly,

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:06.270
"What I tried to do is use subversive imagery
to make a point about society.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:10.310
I tried to seduce the audience to join [the
movie's] society, but then ask, 'What are

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:11.653
you really joining up for?"'

00:07:11.653 --> 00:07:16.650
1999's The Matrix may be one of the most analyzed
sci-fi films ever made.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:21.570
From the film's trans allegory to its prescient
look at a future ruled by data and consumerism,

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.280
every detail of the film has been pored over
since its release.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:29.770
And the costume of arguably the film's most
important character, the Oracle, hasn't escaped notice.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.450
Depicted as an older, motherly woman, the
Oracle can see Neo's future.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.256
"You're the Oracle?"

00:07:35.256 --> 00:07:36.536
"Bingo."

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.070
She appears in an ordinary suburban kitchen
dressed in a green blouse and an orange apron,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:44.960
but this simple costume actually mirrors the
work of renaissance master Michelangelo.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:49.000
The outfit recalls the Delphic Sibyl, who
appears in Michelangelo's most celebrated

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.070
work, the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel.

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:55.260
The Delphic Sibyl is a seer, one of the most
powerful prophets in the ancient world.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.930
According to legend, she even predicted the
Trojan War.

00:07:58.176 --> 00:08:02.770
Ridley Scott's Blade Runner, based on Phillip
K. Dick's novel, Do Androids Dream of Electric

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:07.810
Sheep?, is a cyberpunk sci-fi movie about
a government agent who hunts down rogue androids.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:11.360
Everything from the dialogue and the sets
to the use of color and lighting is meant

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.680
to pay homage to old detective movies, including
Rick Deckard's look, which is influenced by

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.030
movies of that era.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.810
In a chat with AnOther magazine, costume designer
Michael Kaplan explained,

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:24.750
"We definitely felt that Blade Runner was
of that film noir genre, and we looked back

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:27.139
to the films of the 1940s for inspiration.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:30.919
Deckard was as much a Gumshoe as [The Maltese
Falcon's] Sam Spade."

00:08:30.919 --> 00:08:35.190
In fact, Deckard's costume is an obvious tip
of the cap to the iconic detective, a sort

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.610
of futuristic Spade.

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:40.700
The 2017 sequel, Blade Runner 2049, took a
different route.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:46.040
It's set 30 years later and the world is an
even worse place, so the wardrobe had to reflect that.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:50.010
The clothing in the second film is tougher,
more durable, bulkier, and uglier.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:53.800
"The fashion is like very expressive but linked
with surviving.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:59.419
And how they will incorporate survival gears
in those clothes and making them like fashion statements."

00:08:59.419 --> 00:09:01.523
Designer Renée April told IndieWire,

00:09:01.523 --> 00:09:04.285
"I didn't want costumes with [lots of] zippers
and plastic.

00:09:04.285 --> 00:09:06.476
So my job was to make the characters believable."

00:09:06.476 --> 00:09:11.610
2021's Dune is a sweeping outer space saga
where rival houses battle for control of a

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.090
vast interstellar empire.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.980
Based on the Frank Herbert novel of the same
name, Dune calls for futuristic battle suits

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.020
and outfits that can withstand the environments
on harsh alien worlds, but also formal attire

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:23.100
for the powerful House Atreides.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.670
The architecture of the film is drawn from
a school of design called brutalism, and some

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.340
of the outfits have a similar origin.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:33.250
"For me, I wanted the costume design to feel
like a historical movie.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:35.040
To move away from fantasy."

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.190
In a 2021 interview with The Hollywood Reporter,
costume designer Jacqueline West discussed

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.851
the source of inspiration, saying,

00:09:40.851 --> 00:09:42.970
"You want it simple, stripped-down, utilitarian.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.519
I approached nearly all of the costumes that
way.

00:09:45.519 --> 00:09:50.050
I never wanted anything to look over-reaching,
or overdone and baroque in any way."

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:54.290
West took cues from the Romanov family, the
last dynasty to rule Russia before the revolution,

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:55.290
explaining,

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.450
"I thought their costumes were quite elegant
yet simple."

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:58.877
Why the Romanovs, though?

00:09:58.877 --> 00:09:59.896
It's quite simple.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.931
As West explained to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:10:01.931 --> 00:10:05.820
"I thought they were a doomed family, just
like the House of Atreides, where everything

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.250
was about to be taken away from them.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:10.630
I thought there would be some symbolism for
the audience to see and feel that.

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:13.246
Maybe they would make that connection, even
subliminally."

